<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masked discrete diffusion</em>—a flexible paradigm for text generation in which
tokens are progressively corrupted by special mask symbols before being denoised.
Although this approach has demonstrated strong empirical performance, its theoretical complexity in high-dimensional settings remains insufficiently understood.
Existing analyses largely focus on <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform</em> discrete diffusion, and more recent attempts addressing masked diffusion either (1) overlook widely used Euler samplers, (2) impose restrictive bounded-score assumptions, or (3) fail to showcase the advantages of masked discrete diffusion over its uniform counterpart.
To address this gap, we show that Euler samplers can achieve <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy in total variation (TV) with <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> discrete score evaluations, thereby providing the first rigorous analysis of typical Euler sampler in masked discrete diffusion.
We then propose a <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization</em> (<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>) approach that both removes bounded-score assumptions and preserves unbiased discrete score approximation.
By exploiting the property that each token can be unmasked at most once, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text">MATU</span> attains a nearly <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-free complexity of
<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d\,\ln d\cdot(1-\epsilon^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d\,\ln d\cdot(1-\epsilon^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
This result surpasses existing uniformization
methods under uniform discrete diffusion, eliminating the <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln(1/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln(1/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> factor and substantially speeding up convergence.
Our findings not only provide a rigorous theoretical foundation for masked discrete diffusion, showcasing its practical advantages over uniform diffusion for text generation, but also
pave the way for future efforts to analyze diffusion-based language models developed under masking paradigm.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoogeboom et al., </a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
have recently emerged as a powerful class of generative paradigms, frequently regarded as both complements and competitors to the auto-regressive based language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Whereas auto-regressive models learn the conditional distribution of the next
token given a prefix, diffusion language models approximate the joint distribution of an entire token sequence through a noising–denoising process.
This process transforms a potentially complex data distribution into a simpler prior distribution and then iteratively reconstructs it.
In the forward (<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">noising</em>) direction, tokens are progressively replaced by special mask symbols, thereby mapping the data distribution to a one-hot stationary distribution.
The reverse (<em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">denoising</em>) direction then recovers the original text step by step by estimating discrete scores (i.e., density ratios) over the corrupted samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although masked discrete diffusion has empirically outperformed uniform discrete diffusion (where the forward process admits a uniform stationary distribution) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, analyzing and mitigating its computational overhead in high-dimensional settings remains challenging.
As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Theoretical results. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, most existing theoretical results focus on <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniform discrete diffusion</span>.
In these analyses, Euler-type
samplers approximate continuous-time scores by holding them constant over short intervals, leading
to polynomial complexity in the total variation (TV) distance <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically,
exponential-integrator methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> require
<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> steps, while <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping
methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and their higher-order
variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite> need at least <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> steps.
Notably, uniformization-based techniques offer a promising approach, achieving <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\ln(1/\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\ln(1/\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> complexity by unbiasedly simulating the reverse Markov chain.
In the context of <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">masked discrete diffusion</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite> rigorously examined
<math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-TV convergence, showing that <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping can take <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\epsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> steps to converge and also improves upon the dimensional dependence found in uniform discrete diffusion.
However, their stronger bounded-score assumptions make direct comparisons of algorithmic complexity with existing works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uncertain.
Although uniformization can theoretically reach a complexity of <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\ln(1/\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\ln(1/\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> in their framework, it retains the same <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-dependence as uniform discrete diffusion and has yet to exhibit clear empirical benefits in masked diffusion.
Finally, the analysis of the typical Euler sampler used in most empirical studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is still not fully understood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the theoretical challenges of masked discrete diffusion, we first analyze a typical
Euler sampler that parallels the inference procedures used in many
empirical studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Our findings reveal that reaching <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-TV convergence in masked discrete
diffusion with the typical Euler sampler requires <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})</annotation></semantics></math>
discrete score evaluations. This result stands as the first rigorous analysis
of the typical Euler method in masked discrete diffusion and demonstrates
faster convergence than the <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> under stringent accuracy demands.
We then examine uniformization-based approaches for masked discrete diffusion, where
uniformization converts a continuous-time Markov chain (CTMC) into a discrete-time Markov
chain (DTMC) by sampling random Poisson jump times. This technique preserves the exact
transition structure of the original CTMC and provides an unbiased simulation without time-step discretization error. To eliminate the bounded-score assumption used
in previous uniformization analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
we propose a <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization</em> (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>) method inspired
by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Under <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>, we rescale the outgoing transition rates of
the reverse process according to the number of masked tokens in preceding states,
naturally tighting enforcing boundedness in the discrete score estimator while preserving
the unbiasedness of uniformization-based score approximation.
We prove that <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">MATU</span> can reach the same
<math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-TV convergence at a nearly <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-free complexity, offering a significant speedup from <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\ln(1/\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\ln(1/\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1-\epsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1-\epsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The key insight is that uniformization in the
masked setting explicitly identifies which tokens remain masked and require denoising,
thereby avoiding the redundant denoising attempts that slow convergence in uniform
discrete diffusion.
Our main contributions are summarized as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the first rigorous theoretical analysis of typical Euler samplers for masked discrete diffusion. Achieving <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-TV convergence requires <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> discrete score evaluations, surpassing <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>  in high-accuracy settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a new method called <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization</em> (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>). Unlike simply applying uniformization to masked discrete diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our approach leverages a truncation on the outgoing rate, thereby removing the need for a score-bounded assumption. Moreover, our truncation is adaptive to the number of masked tokens, in contrast to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> which relies on a uniform constant, thus making full use of masked discrete diffusion properties.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By leveraging the property that tokens cannot be unmasked multiple times, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">MATU</span> significantly accelerates convergence on the discrete space <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\dots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\dots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, to reach <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-TV convergence, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">MATU</span> uses an expected number of
discrete score calls on the order of</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\bigl(Kd\cdot(1-\epsilon^{2}/d)+d\ln d\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\bigl(Kd\cdot(1-\epsilon^{2}/d)+d\ln d\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Compared to uniformization-based sampler in uniform discrete diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, this result improves
upon the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\bigl(\ln(1/\epsilon)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\bigl(\ln(1/\epsilon)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> rate and surpasses the linear convergence
limitation. Moreover, the dependence on both vocabulary size <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and dimension <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> aligns
with state-of-the-art performance
 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish the notation and setup for both forward and reverse Markov processes in general discrete diffusion models. We discuss marginal and conditional distributions, the transition rate function, neural-network-parameterized discrete scores (density ratios), and a standard training objective. We also present the commonly adopted assumption on score estimation error, which underlies many theoretical and empirical works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
A comprehensive summary of the notation can be found in Table <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix A Notation Summary ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> of Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Notation Summary ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The forward process notations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we consider discrete distributions over
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any functions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,g:{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,g:{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define their inner product as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left&lt;f,g\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}}=\sum_{{\bm{y}}\in{\mathcal{Y}}}f({\bm{y}})\cdot g({\bm{y}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left&lt;f,g\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}}=\sum_{{\bm{y}}\in{\mathcal{Y}}}f({\bm{y}})\cdot g({\bm{y}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given a target distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a forward Markov process
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}^{\to}=q_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}^{\to}=q_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which converges to a stationary distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math> its marginal at time <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and use
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t^{\prime},t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t^{\prime},t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t^{\prime}|t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t^{\prime}|t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>
to represent the joint and conditional distributions over times <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t^{\prime}},{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t})\,\sim\,q^{\to}_{t^{\prime},t},\quad q_{t^{\prime}|t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\,=\,q^{\to}_{t^{\prime},t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})/{q_{t}^{\to}({\bm{y}})}\quad\text{for }t^{\prime}&gt;t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t^{\prime}},{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t})\,\sim\,q^{\to}_{t^{\prime},t},\quad q_{t^{\prime}|t}^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\,=\,q^{\to}_{t^{\prime},t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})/{q_{t}^{\to}({\bm{y}})}\quad\text{for }t^{\prime}&gt;t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Both masked and uniform discrete diffusion models treat this forward process as a time-homogeneous CTMC with transition rate function
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
which denotes the instantaneous transition rate from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\coloneqq\lim_{\Delta t\rightarrow 0}\left[(q^{\to}_{\Delta t|0}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}^{\prime})-\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}}))/{\Delta t}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\coloneqq\lim_{\Delta t\rightarrow 0}\left[(q^{\to}_{\Delta t|0}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}^{\prime})-\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}}))/{\Delta t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
We further define
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})\coloneqq\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})\coloneqq\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
as the outgoing rate, which denotes the instantaneous transition rate from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to all other feasible states.
Under this condition,
the discrete forward process follows</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}q^{\to}_{t|s}}{\mathrm{d}t}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}_{0})=\left&lt;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},\cdot),q^{\to}_{t|s}(\cdot|{\bm{y}}_{0})\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}},\quad\frac{\mathrm{d}q^{\to}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}({\bm{y}})=\left&lt;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},\cdot),q^{\to}_{t}(\cdot)\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}q^{\to}_{t|s}}{\mathrm{d}t}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}_{0})=\left&lt;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},\cdot),q^{\to}_{t|s}(\cdot|{\bm{y}}_{0})\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}},\quad\frac{\mathrm{d}q^{\to}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}({\bm{y}})=\left&lt;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},\cdot),q^{\to}_{t}(\cdot)\right&gt;_{{\mathcal{Y}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">More details and derivation can be found in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B The Markov Processes of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The reverse process notations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To sample from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{*}=q_{0}^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{*}=q_{0}^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math>, discrete diffusion models define a reverse process
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}_{t}^{\leftarrow}\sim q_{t}^{\leftarrow}=q_{T-t}^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}_{t}^{\leftarrow}\sim q_{t}^{\leftarrow}=q_{T-t}^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{y}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\leftarrow},{\mathbf{y}}_{t}^{\leftarrow})\sim q_{t^{\prime},t}^{\leftarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{y}}_{t^{\prime}}^{\leftarrow},{\mathbf{y}}_{t}^{\leftarrow})\sim q_{t^{\prime},t}^{\leftarrow}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> (proof in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 The Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B The Markov Processes of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>), this time-inhomogeneous Markov chain satisfies:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The probability mass function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the reverse process follows</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}\,q^{\leftarrow}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}\,t}({\bm{y}})\;=\;\langle R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},\cdot),\,q^{\leftarrow}_{t}(\cdot)\rangle_{{\mathcal{Y}}}\quad\text{where}\quad R_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;\coloneqq\;R^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\,\frac{q^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})}{q^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}\,q^{\leftarrow}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}\,t}({\bm{y}})\;=\;\langle R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},\cdot),\,q^{\leftarrow}_{t}(\cdot)\rangle_{{\mathcal{Y}}}\quad\text{where}\quad R_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;\coloneqq\;R^{\to}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\,\frac{q^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})}{q^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the reverse transition function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> arises as the infinitesimal operator of the reverse process:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;\coloneqq\;\lim_{\Delta t\rightarrow 0}\Bigl[\,(q^{\leftarrow}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}\mid{\bm{y}}^{\prime})-\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}}))/{\Delta t}\Bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;\coloneqq\;\lim_{\Delta t\rightarrow 0}\Bigl[\,(q^{\leftarrow}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}\mid{\bm{y}}^{\prime})-\delta_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}({\bm{y}}))/{\Delta t}\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">while the outgoing rate is
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under this formulation, the reverse transition rate <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the forward transition rate <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discrete score</em>, defined as the density ratio
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}})}/{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}})}/{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since this ratio is generally intractable, it is approximated in practice by a neural network <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)\;\approx\;v_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)\;=\;q_{t}^{\leftarrow}(\cdot)/{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)\;\approx\;v_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)\;=\;q_{t}^{\leftarrow}(\cdot)/{q_{t}^{\leftarrow}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">yielding an approximate reverse transition rate <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}^{\leftarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}^{\leftarrow}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
To train <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, one typically uses the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">score entropy</em> loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{\text{SE}}(\tilde{v})\;=\;\frac{1}{T}\,\int_{0}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}_{t}\sim q^{\to}_{t}}\Biggl[\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}R^{\to}({\mathbf{y}}_{t},{\bm{y}})\,D_{\mathrm{\phi}}\left(v_{T-t,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\big\|\tilde{v}_{T-t,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\right)\Biggr]\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo lspace="0.337em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{SE}}(\tilde{v})\;=\;\frac{1}{T}\,\int_{0}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}_{t}\sim q^{\to}_{t}}\Biggl[\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}R^{\to}({\mathbf{y}}_{t},{\bm{y}})\,D_{\mathrm{\phi}}\left(v_{T-t,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\big\|\tilde{v}_{T-t,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\right)\Biggr]\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{\phi}}\left(\cdot\big\|\cdot\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{\phi}}\left(\cdot\big\|\cdot\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Bregman divergence associated with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(c)=c\ln c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(c)=c\ln c</annotation></semantics></math>.
As in continuous diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, practitioners often replace <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{SE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{SE}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicit</em> or <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">denoising score entropy</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for more tractable optimization but invariant minimum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General Assumptions.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze both convergence properties and the computational effort required for achieving TV distance convergence in practical settings, we assume the score entropy loss will be upper-bounded. Formally:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[A1]</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score approximation error.</span>
The discrete score <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from Eq. (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is well-trained, and its estimation error is small enough so that
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{SE}}(\tilde{v})\;\leq\;\epsilon_{\text{score}}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>SE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>score</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{SE}}(\tilde{v})\;\leq\;\epsilon_{\text{score}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This assumption is standard in theoretical inference research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where it is commonly presumed that the score can be trained arbitrarily well such that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for any desired <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we instantiate the masked discrete diffusion from the framework outlined in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We then construct a family of auxiliary distributions that approach the ideal forward marginal distribution exponentially quickly as time progresses.
This construction leverages the forward transition kernel of masked discrete diffusion for any <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;s&lt;t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;s&lt;t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>, and can be used as an alternative to the reverse initialization proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional settings.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we adopt a diffusion-based language modeling framework.
Our vocabulary is <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the mask token.
We aim to generate a length-<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> sequence (sentence) <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,2,\ldots,\mathrm{K}\}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The number of mask tokens in specific sentence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hamming distance between two sentences (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) are denoted as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\;\coloneqq\;\sum_{i=1}^{d}\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\quad\text{and}\quad\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\delta_{{\bm{y}}_{i}}({\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">≔</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\;\coloneqq\;\sum_{i=1}^{d}\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\quad\text{and}\quad\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\sum_{i=1}^{d}\delta_{{\bm{y}}_{i}}({\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">respectively.
Generally, we suppose the mask token is never observed in target distribution:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[A2]</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No mask in the target distribution.</span>
The target distribution <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}^{\to}=q_{*}\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}^{\to}=q_{*}\colon{\mathcal{Y}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns positive probability only to those sequences without any mask tokens, i.e. <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{*}({\bm{y}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{*}({\bm{y}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We begin by specifying the absorbing forward transition rate function for masked discrete diffusion:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=1\;\text{and}\;{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{DiffIdx}\left({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\right)}=\mathrm{K}\\
-K\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{d}\bigl[1-\delta_{K}({\bm{y}}_{i})\bigr]&amp;\text{if }{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}^{\prime}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Ham</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>DiffIdx</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mo>∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=1\;\text{and}\;{\bm{y}}_{\mathrm{DiffIdx}\left({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\right)}=\mathrm{K}\\
-K\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{d}\bigl[1-\delta_{K}({\bm{y}}_{i})\bigr]&amp;\text{if }{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}^{\prime}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{DiffIdx}\left({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>DiffIdx</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{DiffIdx}\left({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the single coordinate where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> differ.
Under this transition rule, each non-masked coordinate tends to become masked at an exponential rate.
Concretely, for any <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;s&lt;t&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;s&lt;t&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward transition kernel satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q^{\to}_{t|s}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\prod_{i=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q^{\to}_{t|s}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=\prod_{i=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})+\left(1-\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\right)\cdot\delta_{0}({\bm{y}}_{i}-{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\cdot e^{-(t-s)}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})+\left(1-\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\right)\cdot\delta_{0}({\bm{y}}_{i}-{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\cdot e^{-(t-s)}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left.+\left(1-\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\right)\cdot\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\cdot(1-e^{-(t-s)})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.+\left(1-\delta_{(\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K})}({\bm{y}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})\right)\cdot\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\cdot(1-e^{-(t-s)})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">as shown in Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma B.5 (Forward transition kernel). ‣ B.3 The Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B The Markov Processes of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>.
To approximate the forward marginal distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we exploit this
exponential decay by modeling each non-mask coordinate under a uniform distribution and masking
coordinates at a constant rate. Specifically, we define</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{q}_{t}({\bm{y}})\;\propto\;\prod_{i=1}^{d}\exp\!\Bigl(-t\cdot\bigl[1-\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\bigr]\Bigr)\;=\;\exp\!\Bigl(-\,t\cdot\bigl[d-\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\bigr]\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">∝</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>numK</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{t}({\bm{y}})\;\propto\;\prod_{i=1}^{d}\exp\!\Bigl(-t\cdot\bigl[1-\delta_{\mathrm{K}}({\bm{y}}_{i})\bigr]\Bigr)\;=\;\exp\!\Bigl(-\,t\cdot\bigl[d-\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\bigr]\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> factorizes over coordinates and is straightforward to sample from.
Moreover, as established in Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Exponentially decreasing KL divergence between 𝑞^→_𝑡 and 𝑞̃_𝑡). ‣ Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the KL divergence between <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases exponentially with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exponentially decreasing KL divergence between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the CTMC <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> has transition rates <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\sim q^{\to}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{t}\sim q^{\to}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the approximation of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}\left(q_{t}^{\to}\big\|\tilde{q}_{t}\right)\;\leq\;(1+e^{-t})^{d}-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}\left(q_{t}^{\to}\big\|\tilde{q}_{t}\right)\;\leq\;(1+e^{-t})^{d}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, to ensure <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}\left(q^{\to}_{t}\big\|\tilde{q}_{t}\right)\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}\left(q^{\to}_{t}\big\|\tilde{q}_{t}\right)\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to choose <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq\ln\!\bigl(4d/\epsilon\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.748em;" width="0.748em"><mi>ln</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq\ln\!\bigl(4d/\epsilon\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Exponentially decreasing KL divergence between 𝑞^→_𝑡 and 𝑞̃_𝑡). ‣ Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the running time <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> required for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{q}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{q}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\to}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\to}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> falls on the order of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\ln(d/\epsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\ln(d/\epsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>.
It precisely matches the forward mixing time for uniform discrete diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and continuous diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> converging to their stationary distributions.
Although the final results exhibit a similar convergence rate, the underlying analytical techniques differ substantially because the one-hot stationary distribution of masked discrete diffusion does not satisfy the modified log-Sobolev condition.
Further technical details are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 The Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B The Markov Processes of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Euler Sampler in Masked Discrete Diffusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first introduces the Euler sampler in masked discrete diffusion, widely used for its parallel coordinate updates when reverse transition can be factorized coordinate-wise.
We then extend it to handle more general reverse marginals with unknown correlations, and show how to control accumulative errors by introducing the exponential integrator as the auxiliary process.
Finally, we provide convergence and complexity guarantees for achieving <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>–TV convergence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Typical Euler samplers and their extensions.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Euler-type samplers have become increasingly popular in empirical studies
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> because their parallel-friendly updates
often run faster than traditional auto-regressive models.
Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the practical reverse process, whose
marginal, joint, and conditional distributions satisfy:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}\sim\hat{q}_{t},\quad(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t^{\prime}},\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t})\sim\hat{q}_{t^{\prime},t},\quad\text{and}\quad\hat{q}_{t^{\prime}|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})=\hat{q}_{t^{\prime},t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})/\hat{q}_{t}({\bm{y}})\quad\text{where}\quad t^{\prime}\geq t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>where</mtext><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}\sim\hat{q}_{t},\quad(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t^{\prime}},\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t})\sim\hat{q}_{t^{\prime},t},\quad\text{and}\quad\hat{q}_{t^{\prime}|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})=\hat{q}_{t^{\prime},t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})/\hat{q}_{t}({\bm{y}})\quad\text{where}\quad t^{\prime}\geq t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A key assumption is that the reverse transition for each coordinate is conditionally independent:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{q}_{t+\Delta t|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\propto\prod_{i=1}^{d}\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+\Delta t|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}]|{\bm{y}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{t+\Delta t|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\propto\prod_{i=1}^{d}\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+\Delta t|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}]|{\bm{y}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the token revision function</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{y}}[S\colon\rightarrow Y^{\prime}\subseteq{\mathcal{Y}}^{|S|}]=\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\bm{e}}_{i}\cdot{\bm{1}}[i\not\in S]\cdot{\bm{y}}_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{|S|}{\bm{e}}_{s_{j}}\cdot Y^{\prime}_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}[S\colon\rightarrow Y^{\prime}\subseteq{\mathcal{Y}}^{|S|}]=\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\bm{e}}_{i}\cdot{\bm{1}}[i\not\in S]\cdot{\bm{y}}_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{|S|}{\bm{e}}_{s_{j}}\cdot Y^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">indicates that the coordinates of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by the set <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are replaced by
the corresponding values in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, each non-masked token can be updated independently in the reverse-time direction.
Specifically, by discretizing Eq. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the update for the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th coordinate takes the form:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}]|{\bm{y}})=\delta_{{\bm{y}}_{i}}({\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})+h\cdot R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}])\cdot\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}]|{\bm{y}})=\delta_{{\bm{y}}_{i}}({\bm{y}}_{i}^{\prime})+h\cdot R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}])\cdot\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow\{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\}]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},\;{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},\;{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
the definition of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) ensures
that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{\to}\!\bigl({\bm{y}},\;{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\!\rightarrow\!{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\bigr)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}\!\bigl({\bm{y}},\;{\bm{y}}[\{i\}\!\rightarrow\!{\bm{y}}^{\prime}_{i}\bigr)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow k]|{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}^{(i)}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}[\{i\}\rightarrow k]|{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-mask
token <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling all coordinates to be updated in parallel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, if the assumption in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In Typical Euler samplers and their extensions. ‣ 4 Euler Sampler in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) does not hold, parallel updates become invalid. A practical alternative is to discretize Eq. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) jointly, leading to the sequential update:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\propto\delta_{{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})+h\cdot\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})=\delta_{{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})+h\cdot R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\cdot\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{t+h|t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}|{\bm{y}})\propto\delta_{{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})+h\cdot\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})=\delta_{{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})+h\cdot R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\cdot\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> only if <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Ham</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ham}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})=1</annotation></semantics></math> (see Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>)). Consequently, at most one masked token could be denoised per update. In the subsequent analysis, we consider the Euler sampler using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Typical Euler samplers and their extensions. ‣ 4 Euler Sampler in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) in this more general setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical results.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the Euler sampler, the construction of the training loss, e.g., <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">denoising score entropy</em>, will be related to the step size <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and share the same minimum with</p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{\text{DisSE}}(\tilde{v})\coloneqq\frac{1}{T-\delta}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\int_{kh}^{(k+1)h}\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}_{t}\sim q^{\leftarrow}_{t}}\left[\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}R^{\to}({\mathbf{y}}_{t},{\bm{y}})D_{\phi}\left(v_{kh,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})||\tilde{v}_{kh,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\right)\right]{\textnormal{d}}t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>DisSE</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext>d</mtext><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{DisSE}}(\tilde{v})\coloneqq\frac{1}{T-\delta}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\int_{kh}^{(k+1)h}\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{y}}_{t}\sim q^{\leftarrow}_{t}}\left[\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}R^{\to}({\mathbf{y}}_{t},{\bm{y}})D_{\phi}\left(v_{kh,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})||\tilde{v}_{kh,{\mathbf{y}}_{t}}({\bm{y}})\right)\right]{\textnormal{d}}t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Correspondingly, to suppose the neural score estimator well approximates the discrete score only requires the following score estimation assumption, milder than Assumption <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="item [A1] ‣ General Assumptions. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A1]</span></a>, i.e.,</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[A1]-</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score approximation error.</span>
The discrete score <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from Eq. (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is well-trained, and its estimation error is small enough so that
<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{DisSE}}(\tilde{v})\;\leq\;\epsilon_{\text{score}}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>DisSE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>score</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{DisSE}}(\tilde{v})\;\leq\;\epsilon_{\text{score}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Then, we summarize the convergence and complexity of Euler sampler (with proof in Section <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix C Euler Discretization Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumption <a href="#S4.I1.i1" title="item [A1]- ‣ Theoretical results. ‣ 4 Euler Sampler in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A1]-</span></a>, <a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="item [A2] ‣ Additional settings. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A2]</span></a> and Assumption 2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite> hold, implement Euler sampler with Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In Typical Euler samplers and their extensions. ‣ 4 Euler Sampler in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), if we require</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=\ln(4d/\epsilon^{2}),\quad h\lesssim\min\left\{\frac{\varepsilon}{K^{2}d^{2}\log(d/\varepsilon)},\frac{\varepsilon^{\frac{3}{2}}}{d\sqrt{\log(d/\varepsilon)}}\right\},\quad\text{and}\quad\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq\tilde{o}(\epsilon^{2}/d)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≲</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>ε</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>o</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\ln(4d/\epsilon^{2}),\quad h\lesssim\min\left\{\frac{\varepsilon}{K^{2}d^{2}\log(d/\varepsilon)},\frac{\varepsilon^{\frac{3}{2}}}{d\sqrt{\log(d/\varepsilon)}}\right\},\quad\text{and}\quad\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq\tilde{o}(\epsilon^{2}/d),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the Euler sampler will achieve <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}\left(p_{*},\hat{p}\right)\leq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}\left(p_{*},\hat{p}\right)\leq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> by requiring iterations to at an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(d^{2}\epsilon^{-3/2})</annotation></semantics></math> level.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-leaping method analyzed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite>,
Euler-based approaches can be more effective in high-accuracy settings
(e.g., <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\leq d^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\leq d^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>). However, establishing a clear advantage over
uniform discrete diffusion remains challenging. Due to time-discretization
errors in discrete score estimation, Euler-based inference incurs polynomial
complexity in both the dimensionality <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the error tolerance
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, which is still be worse than that in uniformization-based samplers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section extends the truncated uniformization sampler of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> to masked
discrete diffusion. We first revisit the core principle of unbiased reverse process simulation
via uniformization. Next, we show that the expected complexity of uniformization-based inference
depends critically on the outgoing rates of the reverse transition, and that masked discrete
diffusion naturally offers smaller outgoing rates than its uniform counterpart, leading to
faster convergence. We then introduce <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization</em> (<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>),
which rescales the outgoing rates to eliminate the bounded-score assumption while preserving
unbiased reverse process simulation. Finally, we provide theoretical results on <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>’s
convergence and computational complexity, and compare these findings with existing approaches
in the literature.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Uniformization and the expected number of discrete score calls.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a time-dependent reverse transition rate <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over the interval <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The evolution of the ideal reverse process for any <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be described by</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\leftarrow}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\;=\;\begin{cases}\Delta t\cdot R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}),&amp;\quad{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}},\\[5.0pt]
1-\Delta t\cdot R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}),&amp;\quad{\bm{y}}^{\prime}={\bm{y}},\end{cases}\quad\text{as }\Delta t\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>as </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\leftarrow}_{t+\Delta t\mid t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\;=\;\begin{cases}\Delta t\cdot R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}),&amp;\quad{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}},\\[5.0pt]
1-\Delta t\cdot R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}),&amp;\quad{\bm{y}}^{\prime}={\bm{y}},\end{cases}\quad\text{as }\Delta t\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">following Eq. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
If the total outgoing rate–denoting the instantaneous transition rate from <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to all other feasible states–is uniformly bounded by some <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})\;=\;\sum_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\;\leq\;\beta_{t}\;\leq\;\max_{t\in[a,b]}\beta_{t}\;=\;\beta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})\;=\;\sum_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\;\leq\;\beta_{t}\;\leq\;\max_{t\in[a,b]}\beta_{t}\;=\;\beta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">then with probability <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\Delta t\cdot\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\Delta t\cdot\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, the particle remains in the same state in each infinitesimal time step, thus requiring no additional score computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this observation, the standard <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniformization</em> method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">van Dijk, 1992</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">van Dijk et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>)</cite> simulates the reverse dynamics over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> by iterating the following two-step procedure in the limit <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample whether a transition occurs with probability <math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t\cdot\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t\cdot\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S5.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.</span></span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If a transition occurs, move <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{y}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{y}}^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{M}}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\;=\;\begin{cases}\beta^{-1}\,R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}),&{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}},\\[2.0pt]
1-\beta^{-1}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}),&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑴</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{M}}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}\mid{\bm{y}})\;=\;\begin{cases}\beta^{-1}\,R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}),&amp;{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\neq{\bm{y}},\\[2.0pt]
1-\beta^{-1}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}),&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Under this update scheme, the reverse transitions of uniformization will be equivalent to Eq. (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In Uniformization and the expected number of discrete score calls. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) exactly and introduce no time-discretization error (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 The convergence of Alg. ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> for details).
Moreover, since the number of transitions (and hence the number of discrete score computations) over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> follows a Poisson distribution with mean <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\cdot(b-a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\cdot(b-a)</annotation></semantics></math>, any tighter bound on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> reduces <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and thereby lowers the expected inference complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By the previous discussion of uniformization, the expected number of discrete score calls over the time interval <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by</p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{w=1}^{W}\max_{t\in[t_{w-1},t_{w}]}\beta_{t}\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle W\rightarrow\infty}}{{\approx}}\int_{t=0}^{T}\beta_{t}\mathrm{d}t," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo rspace="0.111em">≈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{w=1}^{W}\max_{t\in[t_{w-1},t_{w}]}\beta_{t}\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle W\rightarrow\infty}}{{\approx}}\int_{t=0}^{T}\beta_{t}\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{0},t_{1},\ldots,t_{W}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{0},t_{1},\ldots,t_{W}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a partition of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>.
In uniform discrete diffusion, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite> show that the ideal reverse process satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}\coloneqq 2K\cdot{d}\cdot\max\{1,(T-t)^{-1}\}\leq\beta\coloneqq 2K\cdot{d}\cdot\max\{1,(T-b)^{-1}\}\quad\forall\ t\in[a,b]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}\coloneqq 2K\cdot{d}\cdot\max\{1,(T-t)^{-1}\}\leq\beta\coloneqq 2K\cdot{d}\cdot\max\{1,(T-b)^{-1}\}\quad\forall\ t\in[a,b],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">providing a uniform upper bound on the total outgoing rate <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">masked</em> discrete diffusion, Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1 (Bound of the outgoing rate). ‣ The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> (with proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 The Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>) shows that the outgoing rate can be bounded instead by</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bound of the outgoing rate)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a CTMC whose transition rate function <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\to}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In Masked discrete diffusion instantiation and approximation. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Then, for any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the reverse transition rate function satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\not={\bm{y}}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})=R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})\leq\beta_{t}({\bm{y}})\coloneqq\frac{\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\cdot K}{e^{(T-t)}-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\not={\bm{y}}}R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})=R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}})\leq\beta_{t}({\bm{y}})\coloneqq\frac{\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\cdot K}{e^{(T-t)}-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to (<a href="#S5.E16" title="In The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), this bound explicitly depends on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of mask tokens in <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>, it is strictly smaller than the uniform bound in (<a href="#S5.E16" title="In The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). Furthermore, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left({\bm{y}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases monotonically as the reverse process proceeds, which progressively enlarges the gap in outgoing rate between masked and uniform discrete diffusion. Because a lower outgoing rate implies fewer expected discrete score evaluations for each time <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, masked discrete diffusion can be significantly more computationally efficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From an empirical perspective, a central observation is: <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">during inference, masked discrete diffusion only updates (denoises) masked tokens, whereas uniform discrete diffusion attempts to re-denoise tokens that have already been denoised.</em>
Hence, in masked discrete diffusion, particles are more likely to remain unchanged at each step, leading to a smaller outgoing rate (and thus smaller <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) over <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, fewer discrete score evaluations are required, underscoring the computational advantages of masked compared to uniform discrete diffusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we approximate the reverse transition rate <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\leftarrow}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> by a learned neural score <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding</p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\;=\;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\,\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})\;=\;R^{\to}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\,\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as dictated by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1 (Adapted from Eqs. (3) and (4) of ( ) ). ‣ The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and Eq. (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In The reverse process notations. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
Because <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned estimator, the outgoing rate <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> may have no explicit upper bounds, complicating control over the expected number of discrete score evaluations.
To mitigate unbounded transition rates, prior work typically imposes a bounded-score assumption
on <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>, restricting it to remain below a fixed constant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or
to grow as a function of the inference time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>)</cite>. However, such assumptions
can severely impact inference efficiency because the chosen upper bound <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> directly governs
Step 2 of uniformization, as described in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E14" title="In item 2. ‣ Uniformization and the expected number of discrete score calls. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). When <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown,
it can be treated as a hyperparameter. Setting <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> too small may yield an infeasible
probability <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\beta^{-1}\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\beta^{-1}\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, forcing the algorithm to fail; setting it too
large preserves feasibility but inflates complexity in direct proportion to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,
tightening this bounding scheme is crucial for balancing both correctness
and computational efficiency in uniformization-based inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we propose a <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mask-aware truncation</em> scheme to rescale the practical outgoing rate <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This ensures that the non time-discretization property is preserved without additional cost, even when <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> becomes large.
Specifically, consider simulating the reverse process over the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w</annotation></semantics></math>-th) time segment <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{w-1},t_{w}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{w-1},t_{w}]</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming the state at time <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{w-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{w-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}}={\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}}={\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following from the monotonicity of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e^{T-t}-1)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e^{T-t}-1)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{numK}\left(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{numK}\left(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1 (Bound of the outgoing rate). ‣ The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the mask-aware truncation is chosen as <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then we set</p>
<table id="S5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;\begin{cases}\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;{\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})}/{\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})},&amp;\text{if }\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})&gt;\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}),\\[6.00006pt]
\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}),&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}\quad\forall{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\!\neq\!{\bm{y}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">≠</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;\begin{cases}\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;{\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})}/{\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})},&amp;\text{if }\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}}^{\prime})&gt;\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}),\\[6.00006pt]
\tilde{R}_{t}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}),&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}\quad\forall{\bm{y}}^{\prime}\!\neq\!{\bm{y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;-\,\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}\!\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.058em">=</mo><mo>−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}}^{\prime},{\bm{y}}^{\prime})\;=\;-\,\sum_{{\bm{y}}\neq{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}\!\hat{R}_{t,{\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{y}}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">With these truncations, the corrected outgoing rate will be definitely upper bounded by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t_{w}}({\bm{y}}_{t_{w-1}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we obtain a practical and efficient inference algorithm, summarized in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Mask-Aware Truncated Uniformization (<span id="alg1.5.1" class="ltx_text">MATU</span>)</span></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Total time <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, a time partition <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=t_{0}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{W}=T-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=t_{0}&lt;\ldots&lt;t_{W}=T-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t_{1}},\ldots,\beta_{t_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t_{1}},\ldots,\beta_{t_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> set as Eq. (<a href="#S5.E17" title="In Lemma 5.1 (Bound of the outgoing rate). ‣ The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), a reverse transition rate function <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}^{\leftarrow}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}^{\leftarrow}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by the learnt score function <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{v}_{t,{\bm{y}}^{\prime}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             Draw an initial sample <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{0}}=[\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K},\ldots,\mathrm{K}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{0}}=[\mathrm{K},\mathrm{K},\ldots,\mathrm{K}]</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              Choose <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t_{w}}=K\cdot\mathrm{numK}(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}})/(e^{T-t_{w}}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t_{w}}=K\cdot\mathrm{numK}(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}})/(e^{T-t_{w}}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Draw <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\beta_{t_{w}}(t_{w}-t_{w-1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Poisson</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\beta_{t_{w}}(t_{w}-t_{w-1}))</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              Sample <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> points i.i.d. uniformly from <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{w-1},t_{w}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{w-1},t_{w}]</annotation></semantics></math> and sort them as <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}&lt;\tau_{2}&lt;\ldots&lt;\tau_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}&lt;\tau_{2}&lt;\ldots&lt;\tau_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Set <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{0}=\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{0}=\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               Find the index set <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> of [MASK] token appeared in random vector <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               For any <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,\ldots,K-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,\ldots,K-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, update <math id="alg1.l10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with

<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{n}=\left\{\begin{aligned} &{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1}[{\bm{z}}_{i}\colon K\rightarrow k]&amp;&amp;w.p.\ \beta_{t_{w}}^{-1}\cdot\hat{R}({\bm{z}}_{n-1}[{\bm{z}}_{i}\colon K\rightarrow k],{\bm{z}}_{n-1}),\\
&{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1},&amp;&amp;w.p.\ 1-\beta_{t_{w}}^{-1}\cdot\hat{R}^{\leftarrow}_{\tau_{n}}({\bm{z}}_{n-1}).\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.667em">.</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>. 1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{n}=\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1}[{\bm{z}}_{i}\colon K\rightarrow k]&amp;&amp;w.p.\ \beta_{t_{w}}^{-1}\cdot\hat{R}({\bm{z}}_{n-1}[{\bm{z}}_{i}\colon K\rightarrow k],{\bm{z}}_{n-1}),\\
&amp;{\mathbf{z}}_{n-1},&amp;&amp;w.p.\ 1-\beta_{t_{w}}^{-1}\cdot\hat{R}^{\leftarrow}_{\tau_{n}}({\bm{z}}_{n-1}).\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              Set <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w}}={\mathbf{z}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w}}={\mathbf{z}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. 

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical results.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the convergence and complexity of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for approximating <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Combination of Theorem and Theorem ). ‣ Theoretical results. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> (proved in Appendices <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 The convergence of Alg. ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 The complexity of Alg. ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Combination of Theorem <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem D.4 (The convergence of Alg. ). ‣ D.2 The convergence of Alg. ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem D.5 (The complexity of Alg. ). ‣ D.3 The complexity of Alg. ‣ Appendix D Truncated Uniformization Inference Analysis ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumption <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="item [A1] ‣ General Assumptions. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A1]</span></a> and <a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="item [A2] ‣ Additional settings. ‣ 3 The Forward Process of Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A2]</span></a> hold, for
Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, if we require</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=\ln(4d/\epsilon^{2}),\quad\delta\leq d^{-1}\epsilon,\quad\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq T^{-1/2}\epsilon,\quad\epsilon&lt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">score</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\ln(4d/\epsilon^{2}),\quad\delta\leq d^{-1}\epsilon,\quad\epsilon_{\text{score}}\leq T^{-1/2}\epsilon,\quad\epsilon&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the partition of the reverse process satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\epsilon/2d,\quad W=(T-\delta)/\eta,\quad t_{0}=0,\quad t_{W}=T-\delta,\quad t_{w}-t_{w-1}=\eta\quad\forall w\in\{1,2,\ldots W\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\epsilon/2d,\quad W=(T-\delta)/\eta,\quad t_{0}=0,\quad t_{W}=T-\delta,\quad t_{w}-t_{w-1}=\eta\quad\forall w\in\{1,2,\ldots W\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the expectation of iteration/score estimation complexity of Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> will be upper bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K(d-\epsilon^{2}/4)+12Kd\ln d" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K(d-\epsilon^{2}/4)+12Kd\ln d</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">to achieve <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}\left(p_{*},\hat{p}\right)\leq 2\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}\left(p_{*},\hat{p}\right)\leq 2\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the underlying distribution of generated samples.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From the above theorem, Eq. (<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Theorem 5.2 (Combination of Theorem and Theorem ). ‣ Theoretical results. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) might appear to enable exact inference by setting <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, this would require infinite mixing time <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, perfect score estimates (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{score}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{score}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>), and infinitely many intervals <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, which is infeasible.
Meanwhile, although each interval has length <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\epsilon/(2d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\epsilon/(2d)</annotation></semantics></math>—leading to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{poly}(d/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{poly}(d/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> intervals in the reverse process—the total discrete score calls remain nearly independent of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,
since many intervals involve no state transitions (see Eq. (<a href="#S5.E15" title="In The comparison of computational complexity and outgoing rate. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)).
Thus, small intervals are used primarily to match the accurate outgoing rate upper bound, without inflating complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, We provide a complexity comparison in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Theoretical results. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">MATU</span> achieves a SOTA for both the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-free complexity and the assumption without bounded-score estimator.
Compared with existing uniformization-based method, Alg <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Mask-aware truncation and algorithm proposal. ‣ 5 Truncated Uniformization in Masked Discrete Diffusion ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> achieves an <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\bigl(\ln(1/\epsilon)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\bigl(\ln(1/\epsilon)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> speedup, primarily because each token is denoised at most once in masked diffusion, whereas uniform diffusion renoises tokens multiple times.
Formally, masked diffusion leverages the monotonic decrease of masked tokens, which cancels the growing outgoing rate:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex15X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\beta_{t_{w}}(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}})\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\right]\approx\sum_{w=1}^{W}\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{numK}({\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{T-t_{w-1}})]\cdot K\cdot\frac{e^{-(T-t_{w})}}{1-e^{-(T-t_{w})}}\cdot\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">numK</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\beta_{t_{w}}(\hat{{\mathbf{y}}}_{t_{w-1}})\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\right]\approx\sum_{w=1}^{W}\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{numK}({\mathbf{y}}^{\to}_{T-t_{w-1}})]\cdot K\cdot\frac{e^{-(T-t_{w})}}{1-e^{-(T-t_{w})}}\cdot\eta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex15Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{w=1}^{W}d\cdot\underbrace{(1-e^{-(T-t_{w-1})})}_{\text{decreasing factor}}\cdot K\cdot\underbrace{(1-e^{-(T-t_{w})})^{-1}}_{\text{increasing factor}}\cdot e^{-(T-t_{w})}\cdot\eta\leq CKd\cdot\sum_{w=1}^{W}e^{-(T-t_{w})}\cdot\eta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathsize="0.900em">decreasing factor</mtext></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext mathsize="0.900em">increasing factor</mtext></munder><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{w=1}^{W}d\cdot\underbrace{(1-e^{-(T-t_{w-1})})}_{\text{decreasing factor}}\cdot K\cdot\underbrace{(1-e^{-(T-t_{w})})^{-1}}_{\text{increasing factor}}\cdot e^{-(T-t_{w})}\cdot\eta\leq CKd\cdot\sum_{w=1}^{W}e^{-(T-t_{w})}\cdot\eta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the factor <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-(T-t_{w})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-(T-t_{w})}</annotation></semantics></math> keeps complexity low.
In uniform diffusion, the same factor remains but grows with <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(T-t_{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(T-t_{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a higher order overall:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex16X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\beta_{t_{w}}\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\right]\lesssim CKd\cdot\sum_{w=1}^{W}\max\{1,(T-t_{w})^{-1}\}\cdot\eta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\beta_{t_{w}}\cdot(t_{w}-t_{w-1})\right]\lesssim CKd\cdot\sum_{w=1}^{W}\max\{1,(T-t_{w})^{-1}\}\cdot\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Since the integral <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\!(1/t)\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.358em;" width="0.358em"><mo>∫</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\!(1/t)\,dt</annotation></semantics></math> diverges more quickly than <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int\!e^{-t}\,dt," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.525em;" width="0.525em"><mo>∫</mo></mpadded><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int\!e^{-t}\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math> masked diffusion achieves lower inference complexity than uniform diffusion.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison with prior works simulating reverse particle SDEs, where <span id="S5.T1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">[A3]</span> denotes the bounded-score assumption used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying (2024)</a></cite> and <span id="S5.T1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">[A3]+</span> denotes the bounded-score assumption used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2025)</a></cite> which is a little bit stronger than <span id="S5.T1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">[A3]</span> due to the time-invariant requirement. All complexities for TV convergence are achieved by assuming <math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{score}}=\tilde{o}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>score</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>o</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{score}}=\tilde{o}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> and setting early-stopping parameters <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\epsilon/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\epsilon/d</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides, the complexity presented by <math id="S5.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> means the <math id="S5.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ln</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln</annotation></semantics></math> dependencies are omitted.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Results</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Forward Type</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inference Sampler</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Assumptions</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Complexity</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Uniformed</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><span id="S5.T1.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Exponential Integrator</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.4pt;padding-bottom:1.4pt;"><a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="item [A1] ‣ General Assumptions. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ On the Complexity Theory of Masked Discrete Diffusion: From poly(1/ϵ) to Nearly ϵ-Free" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">[A1]</span></a><span id="S5.T1.4.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, <span id="S5.T1.4.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">[A3]</span></span></td>
